[Music]  new research is shedding light on how  children are affected by covid19 a study  in the journal jama pediatrics finds  more than 5 300 children and teenagers  in 22 states were hospitalized with the  disease between  may and november and some children are  facing serious complications among them  inflammation maria villarreal spoke with  one affected family in north texas where  coronavirus numbers  have been skyrocketing full of energy  and affection you'd never guess  eight-year-old van fuqua is bouncing  back from a disease  that left him struggling in a texas  hospital bed less than a month ago  my appetite is back what was it like  before  before it was like i was mostly asleep  like i slept like two naps a day yeah  two naps a day  his mother kate tested positive for  covet 19 back in november and the entire  family quarantined  as a precaution so we really kind of  thought we dodged the bullet  because i was the only one sick and  nobody else had any symptoms  but a month later van was hit with his  own symptoms  fatigue first then a fever of 104  degrees  a rash on his body strawberry tongue and  trouble walking  he tested negative for strep the flu and  two cova tests came back negative  leaving doctors scrambling to figure out  what was wrong he had some really scary  numbers on his labs like his heart was  in heart failure his kidneys looked bad  his liver looked bad  so they were really scrambling trying to  figure out you know  which one of these systems is the  culprit and  none of them were it was all an  inflammation reaction to the virus  the virus was covered and the diagnosis  was misc  short for multi-system inflammatory  syndrome a relatively  rare complication from covid in children  van is just one of more than 9 000 youth  covet cases  diagnosed in dallas county this past  december  one of america's most populated all the  trends are headed in the wrong direction  so i think it's going to be a pretty  dark  several weeks if not months as we move  through this pandemic  dr jeffrey khan heads the infectious  disease team at children's health  hospital in dallas  and says kids now make up 15 of dallas  county's  positive cases how did that happen the  virus is widespread in the community  right now  and it's easily transmissible  particularly in a household setting  so if you have somebody who's covered  positive regardless if they're  symptomatic or not  they enter a household the people in the  household are very high risk for getting  infected  more states across the country are  reporting increases in pediatric covert  hospitalizations  in utah a 5 000 percent increase  arizona 4000 and wisconsin south dakota  ohio and minnesota  are all reporting a more than one  thousand percent increase  dr khan says that means parents should  be on the lookout for signs of misc  the multi-system inflammatory syndrome  it can pop up  after kova 19 is long gone what is so  striking about this disease is that  for the most part these are otherwise  healthy children with no significant  medical history  who are coming in and are quite sick  many of these children are in heart  heart failure  uh require intensive care and we're  seeing more and more of this as  as the the pandemic evolves  an evolution being closely monitored by  health officials  around the country for cbs this morning  mira vial  dallas texas parents you know that  children with  misc may have a fever and various  symptoms including abdominal pain  vomiting diarrhea  neck pain rash bloodshot eyes or just  feeling extra tired important to point  out that the cdc says  not all children will have all of these  same symptoms so it's something to  monitor although rare

